{"context":"- ADR-001's governance predates the v2 standard model: its hierarchy (Architecture Alignment → PRD → ADR → Epic → Story), decision authority, and review criteria do not yet recognize the delivery lifecycle specification or the delivery lifecycle standard as governed artifacts.\n- The v2 identity framing — \"Anvil is the software delivery standard: it defines, distributes, verifies, and enforces how software is built, released, and deployed consistently across projects\" (§1.1) — is a governance decision to be ratified through the identity amendment process, not an inheritance to be quoted (provenance note, §1.1).\n- The Manifesto revision scheduled for Phase 1 must be a governed amendment, not an editorial rewrite (§7).\n- In the three-layer model, the specification sits above the Anvil Runtime Source: ADR-035-governance-and-identity-reframing-amendments.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"1. The delivery lifecycle specification is a governed architecture artifact of the Core repository: authored in Core (§5.2); contract-breaking changes require an ADR (a Core-scale governed event, Manifesto §3.6; versioning policy in ADR-024); consumed by ADRs, the Architecture Overview, delivery lifecycle standards, and the conformance harness (EPIC-013).\n2. Standard governance position: delivery lifecycle standards are governed by their maintainers under the standard lifecycle (ADR-027), outside the core ADR flow; the registry enforces conformance — a standard that violates the specification's contracts is rejected by the registry, not patched by Core (§5.5).\n3. Manifesto/identity amendment process: revisions to the identity document are governed amendments — analogous to the PRD revision","consequences":"- ADR-001 is amended, not superseded: the hierarchy and authority rules gain the specification artifact; ADR-001 remains the governance foundation.\n- The Phase 1 Manifesto revision is governed by the identity amendment process.\n- Standards are governed by their maintainers under the standard lifecycle (ADR-027); the registry enforces conformance.\n- Ecosystem contract-change proposals have a defined entry into the ADR process.","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Editorial rewrite of the Manifesto in Phase 1.** Rejected: the identity framing is a governance decision (provenance note, §1.1); an ungoverned rewrite would break documentation-first governance and history preservation (ADR-001 §8.4).\n- **Govern standards through the core ADR flow.** Rejected: standards are content artifacts owned by their maintainers (§5.5); the core ADR flow governs the contract, not framework content.\n- **No ecosystem proposal channel (status quo).** Rejected"}